#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    vector<int> vec = {4, 1, 2, 1, 2};

    // Outer loop to check each element
    for (int i = 0; i < vec.size(); i++) {
        bool isDuplicate = false;

        // Inner loop to check for duplicates
        for (int j = 0; j < vec.size(); j++) {
            if (i != j && vec[i] == vec[j]) {
                isDuplicate = true;

                // Remove both duplicates
                vec.erase(vec.begin() + j);
                if (j < i) i--; // fix i if we removed before i
                vec.erase(vec.begin() + i);
                i--; // go back to re-check at new position
                break;
            }
        }
    }

    // Now vec should have only the unique number
    for (int j : vec) {
        cout << "Single number is: " << j << endl;
    }

    return 0;
}
```

---

### 🔍 Explanation:
- For each element, search for a duplicate.
- If found, erase both instances.
- Adjust `i` properly after `erase()` since the vector shifts.
- At the end, the vector will contain only the unique element.

---

### 🔴 Why You Should **Not Use** This in Practice:
- Time Complexity: **O(n²)** — slow for large inputs.
- Erasing from a vector is **O(n)**.
- XOR method is **O(n)** with constant space — best choice.

